From: OpenWrt HA build <local>
Subject: [PATCH] owsync: what the review of the patches 050 to 080 found

The auditor's review of the four patches before (turn 7 of the joint
review of 2026-11-27/28) found these, each checked in the code:

- A file that could not be sent still made a session that ended well.
  The sender did not look at what its sends returned: with the link gone
  or the peer stalled it went on, read the end-of-sync the peer had sent
  long before, cleared what it owed that peer and wrote the stamp of a
  completed session. With a peer that does not ask again (an owsync
  without patch 080, a listener, a one-shot session) the file stayed
  where it was. A failed send now ends the session as failed.
- The limit of a file's size was half the message size, but the file
  travels as hex inside a JSON document with its path, which may be
  encrypted: a file within a few dozen bytes of that limit was offered
  and could never be sent, and no session with it would be complete. The
  limit is MAX_FILE_SIZE now, 64 KiB below. A file the peer announces
  that is larger (a peer without patch 060 announces every file) is not
  asked for: with such a peer a session, and the join of a router, would
  otherwise never be complete.
- The lock file was released by closing its descriptor. The program run
  after a change is a child that holds the same open file for the instant
  before it closes its descriptors; until then the lock stood, and a
  session right after (the daemon that follows the one-shot session of a
  join) could find it taken. It is unlocked before it is closed.
- Two nodes that connect to each other at the same moment each got an
  error, "Failed to receive Hello", for what is expected: the peer closed
  the connection because its own session ran. A connection that the peer
  closes before its greeting is OWSYNC_ERROR_REFUSED now, said at info
  level, and the daemon tries again within two seconds, three times,
  before it counts it as a failure like any other (a peer that does not
  know this node's address closes the same way). Only a connection
  closed within two seconds counts as that: a peer that stays silent
  until the timeout, and a greeting that cannot be decrypted (another
  key) or read, are errors that say what they are.
- What a session could not finish (a file not replaced, not sent, not
  what was announced) is a warning the first time and a debug line while
  the same session is being tried again; a file that cannot be put in
  place says why.

Not changed, and why: a change that arrives from one peer is not passed
on to a third node that only the receiver connects to (as before the
series; every node of a cluster lists every other). A file that is all in
the kernel's send buffer when the link goes counts as sent, since the
protocol has no acknowledgement: a peer with patch 080 notices that its
session failed and asks again.

The wire protocol does not change.

diff -ru a/include/common.h b/include/common.h
--- a/include/common.h
+++ b/include/common.h
@@ -21,6 +21,14 @@
 #define OWSYNC_MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE (32 * 1024 * 1024)
 #endif
 #define MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE OWSYNC_MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE
+/*
+ * The largest file that fits a message. Its content travels as hex, twice
+ * its size, inside a JSON document that also holds its path, and the
+ * document may be encrypted: 64 KiB are left for all of that. Half the
+ * message size, as the limit was, let a file within a few dozen bytes of
+ * it be offered that could not be sent.
+ */
+#define MAX_FILE_SIZE ((MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE - 65536) / 2)
 
 #define CLOCK_SKEW_THRESHOLD 60                     /* Max acceptable clock difference in seconds */
 #define TOMBSTONE_TTL_MS (30ULL * 24 * 3600 * 1000) /* Tombstone retention: 30 days in milliseconds */
@@ -39,6 +47,7 @@ typedef enum {
     OWSYNC_ERROR_VERSION_MISMATCH = -7, /* Protocol version mismatch */
     OWSYNC_ERROR_INCOMPLETE = -8,    /* Session ran to its end, but not every file was applied or sent */
     OWSYNC_ERROR_BUSY = -9,          /* Another session is running: nothing was done, try again */
+    OWSYNC_ERROR_REFUSED = -10,      /* The peer closed the connection before its greeting */
 } owsync_error_t;
 
 /* Dynamic byte buffer with automatic growth */
diff -ru a/src/daemon.c b/src/daemon.c
--- a/src/daemon.c
+++ b/src/daemon.c
@@ -44,6 +44,7 @@ typedef struct {
 typedef struct {
     bool pending;           /* A session to this peer is owed */
     uint32_t failures;      /* Sessions that failed in a row */
+    uint32_t refusals;      /* Times in a row the peer closed before its greeting */
     uint64_t next_try_ms;   /* Not before this (monotonic clock) */
     uint64_t backoff_ms;    /* The wait before the last retry */
 } peer_state_t;
@@ -247,11 +248,17 @@ static void *poller_thread(void *arg) {
                 }
                 peer->pending = false;
                 peer->failures = 0;
+                peer->refusals = 0;
                 peer->backoff_ms = 0;
                 peer->next_try_ms = 0;
-            } else if (result == OWSYNC_ERROR_BUSY) {
-                /* Another session runs, most likely one this very peer
-                 * started: no failure, and soon tried again */
+            } else if (result == OWSYNC_ERROR_BUSY ||
+                       (result == OWSYNC_ERROR_REFUSED && peer->failures == 0 && ++peer->refusals <= 3)) {
+                /* A session runs here, most likely one this very peer
+                 * started, or the peer turned this node away because one
+                 * runs there (the two connected to each other at the same
+                 * moment): no failure, and soon tried again. A peer that
+                 * goes on closing the connection is a failure like any
+                 * other, from the fourth time. */
                 peer->next_try_ms = now + spread(&seed, RETRY_FIRST_MS);
             } else {
                 peer->failures++;
